        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, a portable power station can run a space heater, but only if its inverter is rated for at least 1,500 watts continuous, and even then a 1,000Wh unit runs a 1,500W heater for roughly 30 to 40 minutes. On the low setting, around 750W, the same unit lasts a little over an hour. Space heaters are pure resistance loads that turn every watt into heat, which is exactly why they are so hard on a battery: there is no efficiency trick that makes the heat cheaper. If you want to stay warm through an outage on battery power, the better plan is to heat yourself, not the room, with an electric blanket or heated mattress pad that draws a tenth of the power and runs all night.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Can a Portable Power Station Really Power a Space Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Technically, yes. Most plug-in space heaters sold in the US top out at 1,500 watts, because that is about the most a heater can safely pull from a standard 120V, 15-amp household outlet over a long period. Any power station whose inverter is rated for 1,500W continuous or more will run one. Mid-size and large units from the major brands routinely offer 1,800W to 3,000W of continuous AC output, so starting and running the heater is not the hard part.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hard part is the battery. A 1,500W heater on high uses 1.5 kilowatt-hours every hour it runs. A power station is just a battery with an inverter attached, and most consumer units hold somewhere between 250Wh and 3,600Wh. Put those numbers next to each other and you can see the problem before doing any careful math: a heater on high can empty a mid-size unit in well under an hour and a large one in two or three.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make the idea useless. A short burst of heat to take the chill off a bathroom before a shower, a few minutes of warmth in a cold workshop, or a heater on low in a tiny, well-insulated room can be perfectly reasonable uses. What does not work is the plan many people have in their heads, which is running a space heater all night through a winter outage on a single power station. The numbers are simply not there.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical heater on high</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~35 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1,000Wh unit, heater on high</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">85-90%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical inverter efficiency</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-100W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical electric blanket draw</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            So the honest answer has two halves. Can it run a space heater? Yes, with a big enough inverter. Should a space heater be how you plan to stay warm on battery power? Almost never. The rest of this guide shows the runtime math for common sizes, explains why some units refuse to start a heater, and lays out the low-wattage alternatives that actually get you through a cold night.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Many Watts Does a Space Heater Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the label on the heater, not the box. Almost every portable electric heater in the US lists 1,500W as its high setting, with a low setting somewhere between about 600W and 900W. Ceramic heaters, oil-filled radiators, infrared heaters and fan-forced coil heaters all land in the same range because they share the same outlet limit. A 1,500W heater produces about 5,100 BTU per hour of heat, since one watt of electricity converts to roughly 3.41 BTU per hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no such thing as an efficient electric resistance heater in the sense people mean. Every resistance heater turns essentially 100 percent of its electricity into heat, so a marketing claim that one heater is cheaper to run than another at the same wattage is mostly about how the heat is delivered, such as a fan that moves warm air toward you, or a thermostat that cycles the element off. Watts in still equals heat out. That matters here because the battery has to supply every one of those watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The thermostat is the one variable that genuinely helps. A heater with a working thermostat runs at full power until the room reaches the set temperature, then cycles off and on to hold it. In a small, well-insulated room on a mild night, the element might only be on half the time once the room is warm. In a drafty room on a freezing night, it may never shut off at all. Your real average draw depends far more on the room and the weather than on the heater itself.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical high</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical low</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceramic fan heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">750-900W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heats quickly, cycles often</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-filled radiator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600-900W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow to warm, holds heat after shutoff</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Infrared / quartz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">750W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Warms people and objects in its line of sight</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small personal heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">n/a</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under-desk and foot-warmer models</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Small personal heaters rated at 200W to 500W are the exception worth knowing about. They will not warm a room, but pointed at your legs under a desk they make a noticeable difference, and they run four to seven times longer than a full-size heater on the same battery. If you are set on using a heater with a power station, a low-wattage personal model is the version that makes sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Will a Power Station Run a Space Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use this formula: usable runtime in hours equals battery capacity in watt-hours, times about 0.85, divided by the heater's wattage. The 0.85 accounts for inverter losses, since converting the battery's DC power to 120V AC typically wastes 10 to 15 percent as heat, plus a little reserve the battery management system holds back. Some units do slightly better and some worse, especially when the inverter is working near its limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run a 1,000Wh unit through it with a heater on high: 1,000 times 0.85 is 850 usable watt-hours, divided by 1,500W is about 0.57 hours, or roughly 34 minutes. On the 750W low setting the same unit lasts a little over an hour. A 2,000Wh unit gets you a bit over an hour on high, and a 3,600Wh unit around two hours. Those are continuous-run figures. A thermostat that cycles the heater off will stretch them, but rarely by more than double.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Power station capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater on high (1,500W)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater on low (750W)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Personal heater (300W)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Won't run (inverter too small on most)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~35 min if inverter allows</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.4 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~35 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.1 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.8 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.1 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.3 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~5.7 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,600Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~10 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Compare that with what an outage actually asks of you. A winter storm outage can easily last 12 hours, and a bad one can run for days. Even the largest single portable unit covers a small fraction of one night on high. Expandable systems that chain extra battery packs to reach 6kWh or more can stretch the time, but at that point you have spent thousands of dollars on batteries to run an appliance that costs about $40.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more effect is easy to miss. Pulling 1,500W from a 1,000Wh battery is a heavy discharge rate, and most units run their cooling fans hard and get noticeably warm doing it. Lithium iron phosphate batteries handle that load well, but you will often see slightly less usable energy at a high draw than the label suggests, and some units will throttle or shut down if their internal temperature climbs too high. Real runtime is usually at or below the formula, not above it. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">power station sizing guide</a> walks through the same formula for every common appliance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Won't My Power Station Start the Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the power station shuts off the moment you switch on the heater, the inverter is almost certainly too small. Many compact units are rated for 300W to 800W continuous output, and plugging a 1,500W load into one trips its overload protection immediately. That is the unit doing its job. Nothing is broken, and switching the heater to its low setting may or may not get under the limit depending on the model.
          </p>

          <p>
            Space heaters do not have the large startup surge that compressors and motors do, because a heating element has no motor to spin up. The small fan in a fan-forced heater adds a few dozen watts. Ceramic heaters can draw somewhat more than their rating for the first few seconds while the element is cold, but this is modest compared with the two-to-three-times surge of a refrigerator or air conditioner compressor. In practice, if the inverter's continuous rating comfortably covers 1,500W, the heater will start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave headroom anyway. An inverter running at 100 percent of its rated continuous output for an extended period runs hot, and some units will derate or shut down to protect themselves. A 1,800W to 2,200W continuous rating gives a 1,500W heater room to breathe. If the heater shares the power station with anything else, such as a phone charger or a light, add those loads too.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RESISTIVE-LOAD MODE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Some units can run a heater above their rating, at reduced power</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Several brands offer a mode that lowers the output voltage so a resistive load like a heater or kettle can run on an inverter rated below its wattage. It works, but the heater produces proportionally less heat. You are effectively running it on a lower setting, which stretches runtime and reduces warmth in equal measure.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Also check the inverter type. Virtually every modern power station from a reputable brand uses a pure sine wave inverter, which is what heaters, electronics and motors all prefer. Very cheap off-brand units and some older inverters produce a modified sine wave, which heating elements tolerate but fans and thermostat electronics may not. If you are unsure what that means for your gear, our explainer on <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pure sine wave vs modified sine wave</a> covers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="better-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is a Better Way to Stay Warm on Battery Power?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat the person, not the room. A room has walls, windows and air leaks that constantly pull heat outside, so a space heater spends most of its energy replacing heat that escapes. Your body is a much smaller target that is already producing heat of its own. Trapping that heat and adding a little more right where you are is dramatically more efficient than trying to raise the temperature of an entire room.
          </p>

          <p>
            An electric blanket or heated mattress pad is the standout. Most draw somewhere around 50W to 100W on a typical setting, and many have timers and lower settings that draw less. On a 1,000Wh power station, a 75W blanket runs for roughly 11 hours, which is a full night. Run it on the lowest comfortable setting, preheat the bed before you get in, and the same unit may stretch further. That is the difference between a cold night and a comfortable one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Warming option</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime on 1,000Wh</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Space heater, high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~35 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Space heater, low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">750W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.1 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Personal under-desk heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric blanket or heated pad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-100W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-17 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12V heated blanket</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40-60W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16-24 hours (no inverter loss)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">USB heated vest or gloves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-15W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Twelve-volt heated blankets built for cars plug straight into the power station's DC car socket, skip the inverter entirely and avoid its losses. USB-powered heated vests, gloves and socks draw so little that a power station can run them for days. None of these warm the house, but they keep the people in it comfortable, which is what matters during an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your home has a gas furnace, the furnace itself is another option, because the gas does the heating and electricity only runs the blower and controls. Many furnace blowers draw a few hundred watts, which a large power station can support for hours. The catch is that a furnace is usually hardwired, so connecting it safely requires a transfer switch or a dedicated furnace inlet installed by a licensed electrician. Our guide on whether a <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator can power a furnace, fridge and sump pump</a> explains the wiring options, which apply to a large battery as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do the free things first. Close off unused rooms and gather everyone in the smallest one, hang blankets over windows and doorways, wear layers including a hat, and use sleeping bags rated for cold weather. Then add a low-wattage heated blanket on the power station. That combination keeps a household reasonably comfortable through a long outage on a battery that could not have run a space heater past midnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can Solar Panels Keep a Space Heater Running?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not in any practical sense. To run a 1,500W heater continuously from solar, the panels would need to deliver 1,500W of real output at the moment the heater is running, plus enough surplus to recharge for the night. A typical portable panel is rated at 100W to 400W, and real-world output usually lands around 70 to 85 percent of that rating in good sun. You would need something like five to eight large panels in full sun just to break even.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter makes this worse. The months when you want a heater are the months with the fewest peak sun hours, often around two to four per day across much of the northern US, with a low sun angle, short days, cloud cover and sometimes snow on the panels. A 400W panel array that might produce 1.5kWh to 2kWh on a long summer day may produce well under 1kWh on a December day. That is less than an hour of heater time for an entire day of collection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where solar does help is with the low-wattage plan. A single 200W panel can often put a few hundred watt-hours back into the battery on a decent winter day, which is enough to recover most of what an electric blanket used overnight. That turns a one-night solution into a repeatable one. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">charging a power station with solar</a> covers panel angles and input limits that make the most of short winter days.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need real space heat through multi-day winter outages, the honest answer is that a portable power station is the wrong tool. A fuel-burning generator running a furnace blower, a properly installed whole-home battery system, or a vented, permanently installed gas or propane heater designed for indoor use are the options built for that job. A portable battery is excellent at keeping the lights, fridge, phones and medical devices going. It is poor at making heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is It Safe to Run a Space Heater on a Power Station?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running a heater on a properly sized power station is no more dangerous than running it from the wall, and in one important way it is safer than the alternative many people reach for in an outage. A battery power station produces no exhaust, so it can sit indoors next to the heater. Never bring a gas generator, a camp stove, a charcoal grill or an outdoor propane heater indoors to make heat. Carbon monoxide from those sources kills people every winter, and the CDC is explicit that they belong outside and away from windows. Our guide on whether <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">portable power stations are safe indoors</a> covers why batteries are the exception.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow the heater manufacturer's instructions as you would on wall power. Plug the heater directly into the power station's AC outlet rather than through an extension cord or power strip, since most heater manuals specifically warn against both. Keep at least three feet of clearance from bedding, curtains, furniture and anything else that can burn, and never leave a heater running unattended or while everyone is asleep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give the power station room to cool as well. Under a 1,500W load its fans will run hard and its vents need clear air. Do not tuck it under a blanket, behind a couch or right against the heater's hot air stream. Many units will shut down to protect themselves if they get too hot, which is safe but means the heat stops when you least expect it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the condition of the heater itself. A heater that has been in a garage for years may have a damaged cord, a cracked housing or a tip-over switch that no longer works. Look for a heater with tip-over and overheat protection and a recognized safety certification mark, and replace any heater whose cord or plug is warm to the touch during normal use. Those rules apply whatever the power source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning to heat a room all night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full-size heater on high drains even a large power station in one to two hours. If your outage plan relies on running a space heater overnight from a single portable unit, it will fail before midnight. Plan around heated blankets and insulation instead, and treat the heater as a short-burst tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the inverter rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A unit with a 600W or 1,000W inverter will shut off the moment a 1,500W heater turns on. Check the continuous AC output rating before an outage, not during one. If you want to run a heater at all, look for at least 1,800W continuous to give the inverter comfortable headroom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the formula without losses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dividing capacity by heater watts overstates runtime. Inverter losses of 10 to 15 percent, a battery reserve and heavy-draw heat all shave real energy. Multiply capacity by about 0.85 before dividing, and expect the actual result to land at or slightly below that figure in cold weather.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the heater through an extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most heater manuals say to plug directly into an outlet, and that applies to a power station too. Thin extension cords and power strips can overheat under a continuous 1,500W load. Put the power station close to where you need heat and plug the heater straight into its AC outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bringing a fuel heater indoors instead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When the battery runs low, it is tempting to switch to a camp heater, grill or generator inside the house. Those produce carbon monoxide and kill people every winter. Keep anything that burns fuel outdoors unless it is a vented appliance designed and installed for indoor use.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a power station do I need to run a 1,500W heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need an inverter rated for at least 1,500W continuous, ideally 1,800W or more for headroom. For runtime, every hour on high needs roughly 1,750Wh of battery once inverter losses are included. That means even a 3,600Wh unit gives you only about two hours, which is why heaters are best used in short bursts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 500W power station run a small space heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a personal heater rated under the inverter's continuous limit, typically 200W to 400W. A standard heater on its low setting usually draws 600W to 900W, which will trip a 500W inverter. Personal heaters warm your legs or hands rather than the room, so set expectations accordingly before relying on one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will an electric blanket run on a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most electric blankets draw roughly 50W to 100W depending on the setting. On a 1,000Wh unit, after inverter losses, that works out to about 8 to 17 hours, enough for a full night. A 12V blanket on the DC car socket avoids inverter losses and usually runs even longer on the same battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an oil-filled radiator better for a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not meaningfully. An oil-filled radiator draws the same 1,500W on high as any other heater and converts electricity to heat at the same rate. Its advantage is that the oil holds heat after the element cycles off, which feels gentler. It does not stretch battery runtime in any significant way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a space heater on a power station while it charges?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many units support pass-through charging, so you can run a load while plugged into the wall or solar. With wall power available, though, you would normally plug the heater into the wall directly. On solar, the heater usually draws far more than the panels supply, so the battery still drains, just more slowly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a space heater damage a power station battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, as long as the load is within the inverter's rating. A heavy draw makes the unit run warm and its fans loud, and frequent deep discharges add to cycle count. LiFePO4 batteries are rated for thousands of cycles, so occasional heater use has little effect on lifespan. Keep the vents clear so it can cool itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest way to stay warm during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Insulation and layers cost nothing to use: gather in one small room, cover windows, wear hats and use warm sleeping bags. Add a low-wattage heated blanket on a power station for comfort. If you have a gas furnace, a properly installed transfer switch lets a generator or large battery run the blower.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

