        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A portable power station can run a space heater if its inverter is rated for 1,500W or more, but a full-power heater drains a 1,000Wh unit in about half an hour and even a large unit in a couple of hours. Use the formula of capacity times 0.85 divided by heater watts to see exactly what your unit can do, and treat any heater as a short-burst tool rather than an overnight plan.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For real warmth through an outage, put the battery to work on low-wattage gear: an electric blanket or heated pad at 50W to 100W runs all night on a mid-size unit, and a solar panel can put that energy back the next day. Keep fuel-burning heat sources outdoors, plug the heater directly into the unit, and leave the big heating job to a furnace, a generator or a system built for it.
          </p>
        </div>

